name: A1_carry_seasonal_v2 (r3, charter 2+3 blend)
members (EW 1/3 each, T+1, 6bps one-side, member vol-target 10% trailing expanding shift1 cap1.0; book re-target 8% trailing expanding shift1 cap3x):
  1) crude-brent spread z-fade HONEST: z126 on spread, |z|>1 enter / <0.75 exit, pos(t)=sign(-z(t-1)), earns spr(t+1)-spr(t). Per A2 r2: EIA daily prints publish next morning; attainable edge after 1-day information lag = SR 0.41 (H1 0.40/H2 0.42). Apr-2020 negative WTI print treated as unpriceable (flat).
  2) natgas winter seasonality: long natgas Nov-Feb only, leg-vol sized, cash (tbill) accrual on flat months. Standalone SR 0.47, 4 neg years.
  3) gold carry-trend: long xau when 252d & 84d trend up else tbill accrual. Standalone SR 0.78 (H1 0.47/H2 1.01), 5 neg years.
rationale: three near-orthogonal premium/timing books (pairwise corr 0.00-0.03): relative-value energy spread harvest, heating-demand seasonality, and monetary-premium gold trend. EW blend + single trailing book vol target keeps worst calendar year flat-to-positive; every member earns a structural premium, and the timing overlays (z-fade entry, trend gate) are what scramble rejects (null mean SR 0.22 vs real 0.82).
validation (window 2008-01-03..2026-07-31, n=4845): SR=0.82 H1=0.57 H2=1.01 ann=8.3% vol=10.1% MaxDD=-12.7% (2021 natgas-vol drawdown; no calendar-year loss) negyears=0/19. plateau: 14 neighbors (weights +-0.1 grid 6, zwin 63/252, gold L 126/315, natgas months variants, book tgt 7%/9%) min ratio 0.90, max 1.09. scramble: 500 draws rotating each member's position vector through identical honest pipelines (seed 20260828): null mean SR 0.22, p95 0.47, p=0.0000. corr to library: CTA_15vol 0.02, Global_Macro 0.02, MS_SharpeW 0.05, L_S_130_30 0.05, RP 0.03, saved A1_mix 0.32.
honesty notes: two lookahead variants caught and discarded during construction: (a) pos.shift(1) earning spr(t)-spr(t-1) = booking pre-publication move (SR 1.71, lag-2 collapse to 0.41 exposes it); (b) same with A2's zero-lag. Saved sleeve uses only t+1-realizable returns.
